
  This matters because it's the pattern every future form inherits. Right now the repo has two incompatible module
  worlds: [38;5;153mindex.html:13[39m loads [38;5;153mapp.js[39m as a [1mplain classic script[0m (globals, no module system), while [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m and
  [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m use [1mCommonJS[0m [38;5;153mrequire[39m/[38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m for Node. There's no bundler and no build step. A shared ID module
  has to run in the browser, so the CommonJS side can't be reused as-is.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how the shared module gets loaded, which fixes the import style every later form must follow.

  - [1mSecond classic script + namespace global[0m — [38;5;153mtracking.js[39m sets [38;5;153mwindow.Tracking = { getUserId }[39m, added as a [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag
    before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m. Matches exactly what [38;5;153mapp.js[39m already is, zero build step, and [38;5;153mindex.html[39m still opens by
    double-clicking. Costs: one global name, and script-tag ordering becomes load-bearing — a future form that forgets
    the tag fails at runtime, not at build time.
  - [1mES modules[0m — [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m with real [38;5;153mimport[39m/[38;5;153mexport[39m. Properly scoped, explicit dependencies, a missing
    import fails loudly. No build step needed; browsers do this natively. The real cost: module scripts are blocked by
    CORS over [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so you'd need to serve the page over a local HTTP server from then on. For a static demo page
    that's a genuine workflow change.
  - [1mBundler[0m — rejecting this outright. Nothing here justifies a build pipeline, and [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no dependencies at
    all.

  [1mMy recommendation: classic script + a single namespace global.[0m Your own guideline is to match existing project style
  before introducing new patterns, and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is already a classic global script — this is the local pattern. The
  global-namespace cost is real but negligible at two or three modules, and migrating to ESM later is mechanical if the
  app outgrows it. I'd rather not impose "you must now run a web server to open the page" on a repo that doesn't need
  one yet.
